<first_data>
Libavformat is a part of the FFmpeg project that provides a means to work with various multimedia container formats. To turn RTP packets from memory into codec packets (also known as AVPackets in FFmpeg), you need to demux the RTP packets as you would with any other container format.

The general steps to do this with libavformat are:

1. Register all available muxers, demuxers, and protocols with `avformat_network_init()` (for network protocols).

2. Allocate and set up an `AVFormatContext` for the input (the RTP stream in this case).

3. Open the input stream using `avformat_open_input()`. This can be tricky with RTP packets in memory because `avformat_open_input()` usually expects a filename or a URL. To handle memory input, you might need to implement a custom AVIOContext.

4. Read packets from the stream using `av_read_frame()`.

5. Decode the read packets with a codec using the libavcodec library.

Here is a pseudocode outline of how this process might look:

```c
// Initialize network components (if required)
avformat_network_init();

// Allocate the AVFormatContext
AVFormatContext *fmt_ctx = NULL;

// Set up your custom AVIOContext if you are reading from memory
unsigned char *avio_ctx_buffer = NULL;
size_t avio_ctx_buffer_size = 4096; // Just as an example
avio_ctx_buffer = av_malloc(avio_ctx_buffer_size);
if (!avio_ctx_buffer) {
    // Handle error
}
AVIOContext *avio_ctx = avio_alloc_context(avio_ctx_buffer, avio_ctx_buffer_size,
                                           0, user_data, &read_packet, NULL, NULL);
if (!avio_ctx) {
    // Handle error
}

fmt_ctx->pb = avio_ctx;

// Open the input stream
if (avformat_open_input(&fmt_ctx, "Your RTP stream source", NULL, NULL) < 0) {
    // Handle error
}

// Find stream info
if (avformat_find_stream_info(fmt_ctx, NULL) < 0) {
    // Handle error
}

// Find the decoder for the stream
AVCodec *codec = NULL;
AVCodecContext *codec_ctx = NULL;
int stream_index = av_find_best_stream(fmt_ctx, AVMEDIA_TYPE_VIDEO, -1, -1, &codec, 0);
if (stream_index < 0) {
    // Handle error
}

// Allocate a codec context for the decoder
codec_ctx = avcodec_alloc_context3(codec);
if (!codec_ctx) {
    // Handle error
}

// Copy codec parameters from input stream to output codec context
if (avcodec_parameters_to_context(codec_ctx, fmt_ctx->streams[stream_index]->codecpar) < 0) {
    // Handle error
}

// Initialize the codec context to use the decoder
if (avcodec_open2(codec_ctx, codec, NULL) < 0) {
    // Handle error
}

// Read frames from the stream
AVPacket pkt;
while (av_read_frame(fmt_ctx, &pkt) >= 0) {
    if (pkt.stream_index == stream_index) {
        // Here you have the packet, and you can process it (decode or whatever you need)
    }
    av_packet_unref(&pkt);
}

// Clean up
avformat_close_input(&fmt_ctx);
avcodec_free_context(&codec_ctx);
av_freep(&avio_ctx_buffer);
avio_context_free(&avio_ctx);
avformat_network_deinit();
```

Please note that the above example assumes that you have a way to read RTP packets from memory. The `read_packet` function that you pass to `avio_alloc_context` must be implemented by you to handle the actual reading of data from your memory buffer into FFmpeg.

Also, error handling is very important but not detailed here; you should handle all potential errors appropriately in your actual implementation.

Keep in mind that working with RTP streams can be complex, and the specifics of your implementation might require additional logic to handle things like RTP headers, packet reordering, or depacketization if you're dealing with a codec that requires it (like H.264 or H.265).
