<first_data>
Certainly! Below is an example of how you can achieve this using JavaScript and HTML. The video will pause at times defined in an array, and you can call a function to continue playing the video.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Pause Video at Times</title>
</head>
<body>

<video id="myVideo" width="320" height="240" controls>
    <source src="your-video.mp4" type="video/mp4">
    Your browser does not support the video tag.
</video>

<script>
    // Define the times to pause the video in seconds
    const pauseTimes = [5, 10, 15];

    // Get the video element
    const video = document.getElementById("myVideo");

    // Set the video to play
    video.play();

    // Function to pause the video at the defined times
    function pauseAtTimes() {
        let currentTime = video.currentTime;

        for (let i = 0; i < pauseTimes.length; i++) {
            const pauseTime = pauseTimes[i];

            // Wait until the video reaches the pause time
            setTimeout(() => {
                if (currentTime >= pauseTime) {
                    video.pause();
                    console.log(`Video paused at ${pauseTime} seconds.`);
                }
            }, pauseTime - currentTime);

            // Update the current time
            currentTime = pauseTime;
        }
    }

    // Function to continue playing the video
    function continuePlaying() {
        video.play();
        console.log("Video is playing again.");
    }

    // Call the pauseAtTimes function to start the process
    pauseAtTimes();
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

In this example, we have an array `pauseTimes` that contains the times in seconds when the video should pause. The `pauseAtTimes` function iterates through the array and uses `setTimeout` to pause the video at the specified times. The `continuePlaying` function can be called to resume playing the video.

Please replace `"your-video.mp4"` with the path to your video file.
</first_data>

