JavaScript (pauseVideo.js):
```javascript
const video = document.getElementById('myVideo');
const pauseTimes = [10, 20, 30]; // Array of times in seconds to pause the video

function pauseVideoAtTimes(times) {
    let currentTime = 0;

    function checkAndPause() {
        if (currentTime >= times[times.length - 1]) {
            return;
        }

        if (currentTime >= times[times.length - 1] + 1) {
            video.pause();
            times.pop();
            setTimeout(checkAndPause, 1000);
        }
    }

    checkAndPause();
}

pauseVideoAtTimes(pauseTimes);
```

In this example, we have an HTML5 video element with an ID of "myVideo". The JavaScript code defines an array called `pauseTimes` which contains the times in seconds at which the video should be paused. The `pauseVideoAtTimes` function is responsible for checking the current playback time and pausing the video at the specified times.

The function uses recursion to continuously check the current playback time and pause the video when necessary. It removes the last time from the `times` array and schedules the next check using `setTimeout`.

Make sure to replace `"your-video-file.mp4"` with the path to your actual video file.

This code will pause the video at the specified times and resume playback when the video reaches the next time in the array.
